---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import portrait from "../assets/liz-bio.png";
---

<Layout title="bio & contact — liz novikova">
  <section class="page-intro">
    <h1>/bio &amp; contact</h1>
  </section>
  <section class="bio-grid">
    <figure>
      <Image
        src={portrait}
        alt="Four passport portraits of Liz Novikova"
        widths={[320, 640, 960]}
        sizes="(max-width: 760px) 86vw, (max-width: 1400px) 34vw, 480px"
        width={960}
        format="webp"
      />
    </figure>
    <div class="contact-block">
      <h2>/contact</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="mailto:novikovaliza262@gmail.com">
            novikovaliza262@gmail.com
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/liz-novikova/">LinkedIn</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.instagram.com/lliza12345678/">Instagram</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="bio-text">
      <p>
        Liz Novikova is an artist, filmmaker and researcher based in Los
        Angeles. Working across moving image, performance, photography and
        writing, she examines how urban space organizes perception and bodily
        behavior. Repetition and displacement are recurring methods in her work:
        an ordinary gesture becomes strange when it is performed elsewhere or
        once too often. She uses kitsch and irony to test the distance between a
        city’s image of itself and the life that unfolds on its streets.
      </p>
      <p>
        After moving to Los Angeles, Novikova returned to Moscow in a foil hat
        and glittering pink and purple clothes, asking passersby whether she
        looked like a tourist. She calls this practice of taking the city’s
        visual codes onto her own body “wearing the city.” Her research on
        experimental film and artist-run spaces in Southern California likewise
        attends to the conditions in which art is encountered, from the site of
        a screening to the social arrangements around it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Novikova is senior editor at ST.ART and works in gallery education at
        the Hammer Museum. Both are sites for her ongoing interest in how a
        work’s meaning changes through public interpretation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .bio-grid {
    grid-template-areas: "portrait text" "contact text";
    grid-template-rows: auto 1fr;
    align-items: start;
    row-gap: 24px;
  }

  figure {
    grid-area: portrait;
  }
  .bio-text {
    grid-area: text;
  }
  .bio-grid .contact-block {
    grid-area: contact;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .contact-block h2 {
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1100px) {
    .bio-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(260px, 360px) minmax(0, 540px) minmax(
          215px,
          1fr
        );
      grid-template-areas: "portrait text contact";
      grid-template-rows: auto;
      column-gap: clamp(32px, 4.5vw, 72px);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .bio-grid {
      grid-template-areas: "portrait" "contact" "text";
      grid-template-rows: auto;
    }
    .bio-grid figure {
      width: min(72vw, 260px);
    }
  }

  .bio-grid p + p {
    margin-top: 1em;
  }
  .contact-block ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .contact-block a {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.18em;
  }
</style>
